%I A399527 #13 Sep 01 2026 10:36:30
%S A399527 3,8,32,77,276,490
%N A399527 The length of the longest possible run of successive prime(n)-rough numbers that are all squarefree.
%C A399527 A p-rough number is a positive integer that is not divisible by any prime less than p. For example, the 2-rough numbers are all the positive integers, and the 3-rough numbers are the odd positive integers. See A399526 for more details.
%C A399527 768 <= a(7) <= 1218.
%C A399527 The least starts of the longest possible runs are listed in A399526.
%H A399527 Amiram Eldar, <a href="/A399526/a399526.txt">Mathematica code for A399526 and A399527</a>.
%H A399527 Amiram Eldar, <a href="/A399526/a399526_1.txt">PARI code for A399526 and A399527</a>.
%H A399527 Eric Weisstein's World of Mathematics, <a href="https://mathworld.wolfram.com/RoughNumber.html">Rough Number</a>.
%H A399527 Wikipedia, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rough_number">Rough number</a>.
%e A399527 a(1) = 3, because the maximum number of consecutive integers ("prime(1)-rough numbers") that are all squarefree is 3 (e.g., 1, 2, and 3; see A007675). No 4 consecutive integers can consist only squarefree terms, as one is always a multiple of 4 = 2^2.
%e A399527 a(2) = 8, because the maximum number of consecutive odd integers ("prime(2)-rough numbers") that are all squarefree is 8 (e.g., 29, 31, 33, 35, 37, 39, 41, 43; see A398951). No 9 consecutive odd integers can consist only squarefree terms, as one is always a multiple of 9 = 3^2.
%e A399527 a(3) = 32, because the maximum length of a run of consecutive 5-rough numbers ("prime(3)-rough numbers") that are all squarefree is 32 (e.g., 1229, 1231, ..., 1321; see A398953). No run of 33 consecutive 5-rough numbers can consist only squarefree terms, as one is always a multiple of 25 = 5^2.
%t A399527 (* See link *)
